 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Credit card rates are far above the cash rate and have barely moved with it. The RBA publishes no recent data for personal loans, so I can't compare those.\n\n**Latest figures (September 2026)**\n- The cash rate averaged **4.36%**.\n- The standard credit card rate was **21.24%**, about 16.9 percentage points above the cash rate.\n- The low-rate credit card was **13.49%**, about 9.1 points above the cash rate.\n\n**How they have moved**\n\n| Period | Cash rate | Standard card | Low-rate card |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jan 2024 | 4.35% | 20.07% | 13.08% |\n| Late 2025 (low point) | 3.60% | 20.99% | 13.49% |\n| Sep 2026 | 4.36% | 21.24% | 13.49% |\n\n- **Cuts in 2025:** The cash rate fell about 0.75 points, from 4.35% in January 2025 to 3.60% by September 2025. Card rates did not fall. The standard card rate rose from 20.78% to 20.99% over that time.\n- **Rises in 2026:** The cash rate rose about 0.75 points, from 3.60% in January 2026 to 4.35% by June 2026. The standard card rate stayed at 20.99% until September 2026, when it rose to 21.24%.\n- **Gap to the cash rate:** The standard card rate sat about 17.4 points above the cash rate at its widest, in late 2025. It is now about 16.9 points above.\n\nCard rates seem to depend much more on lenders' pricing than on the cash rate.\n\n**Personal loans:** The RBA's series for unsecured fixed and variable personal loans has no data for this period, so I can't give a figure for them. These are advertised rates, and rates actually paid are usually lower. The RBA's F6 table has rates actually paid, if you want to check those.",
